        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces every nan with a number you choose (0 by default), and plus and minus infinity with numbers you choose, leaving the rest alone - so a lost sensor reading or a division by zero does not spread through everything downstream.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nan_to_num <nan value>\nDefault: 0.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends. Whole numbers cannot be nan or infinite, so they pass through unchanged.\n\nnan (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat nan becomes.\n\nposinf, neginf (float boxes, also inlets):\nWhat plus and minus infinity become. Both 0 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe values with the replacements made.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0 with nan set to -1: the nan becomes -1, and both infinities 0.\n\nRELATED:\nt.isnan and t.isfinite find the bad values. t.where replaces them with values from another tensor (the last good frame). t.nanmean averages while skipping nan. np.nan_to_num is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces every nan with a number you choose (0 by default), and plus and minus infinity with numbers you choose, leaving the rest alone - so a lost sensor reading or a division by zero does not spread through everything downstream.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nan_to_num <nan value>\nDefault: 0.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends. Whole numbers cannot be nan or infinite, so they pass through unchanged.\n\nnan (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat nan becomes.\n\nposinf, neginf (float boxes, also inlets):\nWhat plus and minus infinity become. Both 0 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe values with the replacements made.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0 with nan set to -1: the nan becomes -1, and both infinities 0.\n\nRELATED:\nt.isnan and t.isfinite find the bad values. t.where replaces them with values from another tensor (the last good frame). t.nanmean averages while skipping nan. np.nan_to_num is the numpy version."
        },
